00:01:23.811 --> 00:01:24.963
"because people have been waiting,

00:01:24.963 --> 00:01:26.293
"and I couldn't disappoint."

00:01:26.293 --> 00:01:29.128
So I apologize for having my energy down

00:01:29.128 --> 00:01:31.228
a little bit for episode 81.

00:01:31.228 --> 00:01:35.487
But India, let's get in

00:01:35.487 --> 00:01:36.633
to the show.

00:01:37.544 --> 00:01:38.734
- [Voiceover] Chris asks,

00:01:38.734 --> 00:01:40.939
"You've done two long-format web shows,

00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:43.534
"in a world where gurus
advise keeping online videos

00:01:43.534 --> 00:01:44.864
"short and sweet.

00:01:44.864 --> 00:01:45.984
"What gives?"

00:01:46.996 --> 00:01:48.536
- Chris, a couple of things.

00:01:48.536 --> 00:01:51.896
First and foremost, it's
always very dangerous

00:01:51.896 --> 00:01:55.608
to listen to the advice
of online marketing gurus.

00:01:58.015 --> 00:02:01.375
I think what gives, is very
simply, something that I think

00:02:01.375 --> 00:02:05.500
is the right advice, which
is quality trumps everything.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:06.447
Right?

00:02:06.447 --> 00:02:10.961
And so, I remember in 2007,
being flown out to Mountain View

00:02:10.961 --> 00:02:14.112
in Sunnyvale by Yahoo and
Google to try to figure out

00:02:14.112 --> 00:02:16.772
what gives with Wine Library TV.

00:02:16.772 --> 00:02:18.907
Now remember, this is eight years ago,

00:02:18.907 --> 00:02:21.497
trying to figure out why
my show was doing so well,

00:02:21.497 --> 00:02:23.439
even though it was long.

00:02:23.439 --> 00:02:27.132
And so the real answer was quite simple.

00:02:27.132 --> 00:02:29.057
Quality is quality is quality.

00:02:29.057 --> 00:02:32.172
If I'm able to put out a 15,
20 minute, 30 minute show,

00:02:32.172 --> 00:02:34.902
and I can captivate and hold the audience,

00:02:34.902 --> 00:02:36.932
well, then it has a chance of doing well.

00:02:36.932 --> 00:02:38.644
If I can't, then it won't.

00:02:38.644 --> 00:02:40.219
I mean it's not super complicated.

00:02:40.219 --> 00:02:43.264
There are plenty of shit 14-second videos.

00:02:43.264 --> 00:02:46.169
There are plenty of waste-of-time,
never-get-off-the-ground,

00:02:46.169 --> 00:02:48.092
one minute shows.

00:02:48.092 --> 00:02:50.649
You will watch a three-hour
Avatar, because it's good,

00:02:50.649 --> 00:02:53.169
you will not watch a 15-second commercial

00:02:53.169 --> 00:02:54.341
'cause it's crap.

00:02:54.341 --> 00:02:58.769
So the length is not the
variable of the quality.

00:02:58.769 --> 00:03:00.974
As a matter of fact,
to break DRock's heart,

00:03:00.974 --> 00:03:04.194
neither is the sound, or the
lighting, or the production,

00:03:04.194 --> 00:03:06.819
it is the message, it is the
message, it is the message.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:08.149
I'm telling you, it's the message.

00:03:08.149 --> 00:03:10.807
Now, DRock has come into my life

00:03:10.807 --> 00:03:12.314
and made everything much better.

00:03:12.314 --> 00:03:15.077
So I do respect the
production more than I did

00:03:15.077 --> 00:03:16.864
in 2006, '07 and '08.

00:03:16.864 --> 00:03:19.244
But the truth is, for the fist pump

00:03:19.244 --> 00:03:21.589
that he just put out behind the scenes,

00:03:21.589 --> 00:03:24.284
the bottom line is, if he
wasn't dealing with this

00:03:24.284 --> 00:03:26.766
kind of quality, all the
production he put out

00:03:26.766 --> 00:03:29.801
still, wouldn't, matter.

00:03:31.663 --> 00:03:33.903
- [Voiceover] Paul asks,
"Can you provide insight into

00:03:33.903 --> 00:03:36.735
"how you nurtured your
public speaking chops,

00:03:36.735 --> 00:03:38.520
"besides just hustling on stage?

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.060
"How do you prepare for a talk?

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:42.792
"Have you ever been
nervous to speak publicly?"

00:03:43.592 --> 00:03:44.675
- Paul, great question.

00:03:44.675 --> 00:03:47.127
First of all, ridiculously rad picture.

00:03:47.127 --> 00:03:49.295
DRock, show it again.

00:03:49.295 --> 00:03:50.907
In love with the drone.

00:03:50.907 --> 00:03:53.952
Just completely caught
our attention, great job.

00:03:56.571 --> 00:04:00.036
The truth is, I hit the
stage somewhere in 2006,

00:04:00.036 --> 00:04:04.234
for the first, first time,
as somebody to listen to,

00:04:04.234 --> 00:04:06.266
and it was instantaneous.

00:04:06.266 --> 00:04:07.421
It was love at first sight.

00:04:07.421 --> 00:04:09.661
I loved the stage, the stage loved me.

00:04:09.661 --> 00:04:11.968
And so obviously, I've
got a lot of practice.

00:04:11.968 --> 00:04:14.733
Matter of fact, what I'm struggling with

00:04:14.733 --> 00:04:15.853
more than anything right now,

00:04:15.853 --> 00:04:17.952
is that I'm getting into such a rhythm,

00:04:17.952 --> 00:04:19.283
I feel like I'm a stand up comic, right,

00:04:19.283 --> 00:04:21.243
that I have my kind of talk.

00:04:21.243 --> 00:04:24.743
And I take a lot of pride of
making it eight to 15 minutes

00:04:24.743 --> 00:04:27.088
out of an hour different
based on the audience,

00:04:27.088 --> 00:04:28.838
but I want it to be 45 minutes different.

00:04:28.838 --> 00:04:30.761
But I'm kind of a cadence right now,

00:04:30.761 --> 00:04:32.686
so obviously I've gotten better.

00:04:32.686 --> 00:04:35.610
My timing has gotten
down, but I've never done

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:38.445
anything like, it's kind
of like this show, right?

00:04:38.445 --> 00:04:40.895
Like, one of the things I
love about doing Meerkat

00:04:40.895 --> 00:04:42.925
behind the scenes now
is that people can see

00:04:42.925 --> 00:04:45.305
how little to no editing there really is.

00:04:45.305 --> 00:04:46.985
There is no real prep.

00:04:46.985 --> 00:04:49.645
I think my biggest secret
is talk about what you know

00:04:49.645 --> 00:04:51.255
you're good at, right?

00:04:51.255 --> 00:04:52.760
Like, talk about stuff you know.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:56.295
I try not to answer questions
about foreign-policy

00:04:56.295 --> 00:05:00.040
or currency or you know, I
stay away from Bitcoin or

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.560
I don't talk about the
things I don't know, right?

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.850
I can spew my opinion and
at the end of the day,

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:07.591
this show and everything
else is my opinion,

00:05:07.591 --> 00:05:11.542
but boy, is that grounded
in really strong research

00:05:11.542 --> 00:05:14.092
and skill and practitioner DNA.

00:05:14.092 --> 00:05:16.792
I feel like my opinion
business matters because

00:05:16.792 --> 00:05:19.012
of the execution I did to get there,

00:05:19.012 --> 00:05:22.102
whereas my opinion on everything
else, I have my opinions

00:05:22.102 --> 00:05:26.047
on a lot of things, but
they're not as grounded

00:05:26.047 --> 00:05:27.217
and as much researched.

00:05:27.217 --> 00:05:28.957
They're more intuitive, which is fine.

00:05:28.957 --> 00:05:31.417
Take them for what they are,
you know what opinions are.

00:05:34.069 --> 00:05:36.961
I stick to what I know, and
that helps me just scream.

00:05:36.961 --> 00:05:39.749
I'm a good communicator by DNA,

00:05:39.749 --> 00:05:43.831
and by staying in my comfort
zone, in my lanes of expertise

00:05:43.831 --> 00:05:45.541
and the places where I
actually spend my time

00:05:45.541 --> 00:05:49.201
honing my craft, it comes
off very clean because

00:05:49.201 --> 00:05:52.121
it's very natural because it is natural.

00:05:53.161 --> 00:05:55.111
- [Voiceover] Kelton asks,
"What would be the best way

00:05:55.111 --> 00:05:57.991
"to sample healthy energy
drinks out to 1000 people

00:05:57.991 --> 00:05:59.829
"by the end of the year?"

00:06:00.841 --> 00:06:02.639
- Kelton, I took this question
because I want to give you

00:06:02.639 --> 00:06:05.671
a shout out and give you
exposure to my audience,

00:06:05.671 --> 00:06:07.231
because I appreciate your hustle.

00:06:07.231 --> 00:06:09.631
On the flip side, your question is crap.

00:06:09.631 --> 00:06:10.651
Let me explain.

00:06:10.651 --> 00:06:14.341
To get 1000 people to try your
energy drink, I don't know,

00:06:14.341 --> 00:06:17.551
just go to a crowded place
outside and start pouring.

00:06:17.551 --> 00:06:20.041
I would have a thousand
people try my energy drink

00:06:20.041 --> 00:06:22.741
literally within eight hours on a Saturday

00:06:22.741 --> 00:06:23.911
by going to Central Park.

00:06:23.911 --> 00:06:25.681
And maybe you live in some rural area,

00:06:25.681 --> 00:06:27.601
so get your ass into a car or a train

00:06:27.601 --> 00:06:29.251
and find a crap load of people.

00:06:29.251 --> 00:06:32.129
Get a goddamn stand and pour
to a bunch of little cups

00:06:32.129 --> 00:06:33.841
and you'll hit your
thousand in eight hours.

00:06:33.841 --> 00:06:35.023
Shit question.

00:06:36.085 --> 00:06:37.945
- Gary, it's magician and
corporate entertainer,

00:06:37.945 --> 00:06:38.995
David Ranalli here.

00:06:38.995 --> 00:06:41.215
What's the deal with
Facebook's video push?

00:06:41.215 --> 00:06:42.505
Do you think they're going
to become a monetized

00:06:42.505 --> 00:06:43.705
video platform?

00:06:43.705 --> 00:06:45.445
And what does that mean
for people who are starting

00:06:45.445 --> 00:06:48.205
a YouTube show in the
coming weeks like I am?

00:06:48.205 --> 00:06:49.655
Thanks.

00:06:50.275 --> 00:06:51.985
- David, it means that, first of all,

00:06:51.985 --> 00:06:53.455
to answer your question, yes,

00:06:53.455 --> 00:06:55.315
it means for people
starting YouTube shows,

00:06:55.315 --> 00:06:57.505
they should seriously
start considering starting

00:06:57.505 --> 00:06:58.945
Facebook shows.

00:06:58.945 --> 00:07:01.945
Now that is people that
actually have money, right?

00:07:01.945 --> 00:07:03.955
Because where Facebook
video gets really valuable

00:07:03.955 --> 00:07:06.595
is when you start spending
$200 to $500 a day

00:07:06.595 --> 00:07:09.445
targeting audiences because
you can target at a level

00:07:09.445 --> 00:07:11.215
we've never seen before.

00:07:11.215 --> 00:07:15.595
And so you know, look, I
think Facebook is a massive,

00:07:15.595 --> 00:07:18.535
it's already a massive
competitor to YouTube.

00:07:18.535 --> 00:07:20.515
YouTube should actually be concerned

00:07:20.515 --> 00:07:22.225
when you layer the data.

00:07:22.225 --> 00:07:24.295
Listen, I keep yelling about this.

00:07:24.295 --> 00:07:26.726
The data, the data.

00:07:27.506 --> 00:07:30.180
The data that Facebook
sits on top that allows you

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:34.420
to target against creates
an ultimate machine.

00:07:34.905 --> 00:07:36.935
And you know, I'm spending a ton of time,

00:07:36.935 --> 00:07:38.015
I'm looking at Andy.

00:07:38.015 --> 00:07:39.552
No I'm not, I thought that was Andy.

00:07:39.552 --> 00:07:42.387
I'm trying to look at
Andy K right now, my team,

00:07:42.387 --> 00:07:44.172
who grows audience with me,

00:07:44.172 --> 00:07:46.937
and Facebook video is at
the top, the tippy top

00:07:46.937 --> 00:07:48.512
of our concern.

00:07:48.512 --> 00:07:50.927
And I think now that
they're showing view count

00:07:50.927 --> 00:07:53.132
and now that they're
embedded, one of the reasons

00:07:53.132 --> 00:07:56.247
I like using YouTube is it
shows perception is reality,

00:07:56.247 --> 00:07:57.049
how many views.

00:07:57.049 --> 00:07:58.134
It builds brand.

00:07:58.134 --> 00:07:59.841
Now that I can do the
same thing with Facebook,

00:07:59.841 --> 00:08:03.648
my intentions are to
maybe even switch some...

00:08:05.225 --> 00:08:08.445
If you ask me if my primary
embed was Facebook video

00:08:08.445 --> 00:08:11.035
over YouTube video six months from today

00:08:11.035 --> 00:08:13.665
on Gary Vaynerchuk business
videos, I would say yes.

00:08:14.182 --> 00:08:15.267
Think about that.

00:08:15.267 --> 00:08:18.732
So it's a huge, huge deal.

00:08:18.732 --> 00:08:23.177
It also, I think, competition
breeds innovation.

00:08:23.177 --> 00:08:25.977
And so I'm excited because
I think YouTube gets scared

00:08:25.977 --> 00:08:26.887
a little bit here.

00:08:26.887 --> 00:08:28.357
Google gets scared a little bit here,

00:08:28.357 --> 00:08:31.752
and we'll actually see
better quality innovation

00:08:31.752 --> 00:08:34.552
come out of YouTube because
they've been pretty stale

00:08:34.552 --> 00:08:37.455
for a half decade in a lot of ways.

00:08:37.455 --> 00:08:39.587
And I think that they're
going to both push each other.

00:08:39.587 --> 00:08:42.174
Now you've got live streaming video.

00:08:42.174 --> 00:08:45.919
Video is king, and we're living

00:08:45.919 --> 00:08:47.879
some of the picture revolution, right,

00:08:47.879 --> 00:08:49.751
Instagram at the forefront.

00:08:49.751 --> 00:08:52.201
But I think video still
has a long way to go,

00:08:52.201 --> 00:08:54.301
and we're living through
it, and I just think

00:08:54.301 --> 00:08:56.611
there's more upside, more
in both categories actually.

00:08:56.611 --> 00:08:59.691
But I think Facebook
video is probably grossly

00:08:59.691 --> 00:09:01.039
underestimated by the far majority

00:09:01.039 --> 00:09:02.342
of this audience right now.

00:09:02.342 --> 00:09:03.629
I think the upside is enormous.

00:09:03.629 --> 00:09:05.974
I myself am paying a
ton of attention to it.

00:09:05.974 --> 00:09:07.829
You know what that speaks.

00:09:07.829 --> 00:09:10.172
- [Voiceover] Michelle
asks, "What's your take

00:09:10.172 --> 00:09:12.874
"on subscription box services?"

00:09:15.036 --> 00:09:17.941
- Michelle, first and
foremost, congratulations.

00:09:17.941 --> 00:09:20.216
That is an adorable picture.

00:09:20.216 --> 00:09:23.506
It's really cute, and I fully expect you

00:09:24.131 --> 00:09:26.371
and that little one to send
me a version of that picture

00:09:26.371 --> 00:09:28.961
10 years from today since
I put you on the show,

00:09:28.961 --> 00:09:30.431
and I'd like to see it.

00:09:30.431 --> 00:09:31.901
So I'm looking forward to being surprised

00:09:31.901 --> 00:09:34.876
and trying to recall why it's
happening 10 years from today.

00:09:34.876 --> 00:09:37.116
Big shout out to 2025.

00:09:38.476 --> 00:09:40.438
The answer to your question,
box services, look,

00:09:40.438 --> 00:09:42.573
I wrote one of the first
checks into Birchbox

00:09:42.573 --> 00:09:45.898
and was an angel investor in BarkBox.

00:09:45.898 --> 00:09:48.628
We're incubating a business
here called Faithbox.

00:09:48.628 --> 00:09:51.495
I'm a very big fan of the
subscription box service.

00:09:51.495 --> 00:09:53.385
I think it's the way people like to buy.

00:09:53.385 --> 00:09:55.730
We like to buy once and forget about it.

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:57.582
I think they're good
businesses because then,

00:09:57.582 --> 00:09:59.889
even if we get stuff we
don't want, we don't cancel

00:09:59.889 --> 00:10:01.012
right away.

00:10:01.012 --> 00:10:02.727
So you get an extra two
to five, seven months

00:10:02.727 --> 00:10:04.932
worth of business.

00:10:04.932 --> 00:10:06.927
That's just the truth.

00:10:06.927 --> 00:10:08.647
And so

00:10:09.094 --> 00:10:10.896
I'm very bullish on them,
and I think that there's

00:10:10.896 --> 00:10:12.471
innovation to be had.

00:10:12.471 --> 00:10:15.026
I think that the box was
kind of the innovation.

00:10:15.026 --> 00:10:16.986
All this is, is the month club, right?

00:10:16.986 --> 00:10:18.631
It's just the basket of the month club.

00:10:18.631 --> 00:10:20.941
So I've been thinking
for a long time like,

00:10:20.941 --> 00:10:22.726
what else can I ship it in?

00:10:22.726 --> 00:10:24.826
Can it be like a balloon?

00:10:24.826 --> 00:10:29.650
Like, can it be like peanutballoon.com,

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:31.715
where like a balloon lands
and there's peanuts in it

00:10:31.715 --> 00:10:32.657
each month?

00:10:32.657 --> 00:10:35.597
I feel like the mechanism, the box itself

00:10:35.597 --> 00:10:37.067
has some innovation around it.

00:10:37.067 --> 00:10:39.552
I hope that idea spurred
a thought for somebody

00:10:39.552 --> 00:10:43.332
and they start a huge million-dollar
business because of it.

00:10:43.332 --> 00:10:45.572
I definitely think that
there's a play there.

00:10:45.572 --> 00:10:48.022
As far as upping the hustle, I would say

00:10:49.452 --> 00:10:51.799
the birth of a new child is
one of the most remarkable,

00:10:51.799 --> 00:10:53.339
important moments of one's life.

00:10:53.339 --> 00:10:55.824
And even though I'm 24/7, 365 hustle,

00:10:55.824 --> 00:10:58.797
much like I talk about,
getting plenty of sleep

00:10:58.797 --> 00:10:59.989
and vacations matter.

00:10:59.989 --> 00:11:01.354
It's what you do when you're awake.

00:11:01.354 --> 00:11:05.519
I wouldn't overstress the
first one to 10 months,

00:11:05.519 --> 00:11:07.234
depending on your DNA.

00:11:07.234 --> 00:11:09.334
I would really focus on
laying the foundation

00:11:09.334 --> 00:11:13.744
of a healthy relationship
and lifestyle for your baby.

00:11:13.744 --> 00:11:16.089
You've got plenty of
time to pick it back up.

00:11:16.089 --> 00:11:18.327
Unless you're a maniac like
me, then I would just say,

00:11:18.327 --> 00:11:20.954
just a little less sleep and
try to prioritize your health

00:11:20.954 --> 00:11:24.384
and vitamins and drink
water and stuff like that.

00:11:24.384 --> 00:11:26.449
Don't eat weird food
that makes you throw up

00:11:26.449 --> 00:11:28.788
for seven hours the next morning.

00:11:28.788 --> 00:11:29.943
And that's it.

00:11:29.943 --> 00:11:31.383
Question of the day.

00:11:31.725 --> 00:11:33.265
Let's have India ask the question today.

00:11:33.265 --> 00:11:34.455
I like the on the spot stuff.

00:11:34.455 --> 00:11:36.062
Shift it quickly, don't give her time.

00:11:37.126 --> 00:11:39.808
- Oh, I'm so terrible at this.

00:11:40.763 --> 00:11:42.268
- [Gary] This is so rewarding.

00:11:42.268 --> 00:11:43.703
As a matter of fact, double switcheroo,

00:11:43.703 --> 00:11:45.383
Alex gets to ask the question of the day!

00:11:45.383 --> 00:11:47.644
(laughs)
Yes.

00:11:49.984 --> 00:11:51.216
- How much wood would a woodchuck chuck--

00:11:51.216 --> 00:11:52.936
- [Gary] No, we'll not accept that.

00:11:52.936 --> 00:11:55.645
Back to India, back to
India, back to India.

00:11:56.616 --> 00:11:59.027
- Have you ever had food poisoning?

00:11:59.027 --> 00:12:00.826
That's my question.

00:12:00.826 --> 00:12:03.752
- Jesus Christ, I'll take over.

00:12:06.477 --> 00:12:10.753
Take a picture of your home
screen on your phone right now

00:12:10.753 --> 00:12:13.363
and tweet it to me at @GaryVee.

00:12:13.363 --> 00:12:16.873
I want to see the apps
that dominate your day.

00:12:16.873 --> 00:12:17.893
So you know what, I failed.

00:12:17.893 --> 00:12:19.723
Nobody asked the question of the day.

00:12:19.723 --> 00:12:22.213
It was the dictation of the day.

00:12:22.213 --> 00:12:25.835
You keep asking questions,
I'll keep answering them.

00:12:26.483 --> 00:12:27.985
Wearing around her neck today, what--

00:12:27.985 --> 00:12:29.065
- [India] Scarf.

00:12:29.065 --> 00:12:29.735
- [Gary] That is not a scarf.

00:12:29.735 --> 00:12:31.265
- Yeah it is, it's a circle scarf.

00:12:31.265 --> 00:12:33.845
- [Alex] Yeah, infinity
scarf. Infinity scarf.

00:12:33.845 --> 00:12:35.777
- You just wrap it around your head.

00:12:35.777 --> 00:12:37.427
It's a circle, it never ends.

00:12:37.427 --> 00:12:38.279
I'm cold.

00:12:38.279 --> 00:12:39.119
You know I'm always cold.

00:12:39.119 --> 00:12:40.829
- I know, I'm always hot.

00:12:40.829 --> 00:12:42.291
Tsk.

00:12:42.621 --> 00:12:43.893
(laughter)

00:12:43.893 --> 00:12:45.393
- [Voiceover] (mumbles)

00:12:45.393 --> 00:12:46.713
(laughter)

00:12:46.713 --> 00:12:49.068
- That is not the beta test Meerkat.

00:12:49.068 --> 00:12:51.190
I'm using the actual one
that's in the App Store.

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:52.270
- [Voiceover] All right,
so we should be fine.

00:12:52.270 --> 00:12:53.554
- So we should be fine.

00:12:53.554 --> 00:12:55.066
But great job by

00:12:55.066 --> 00:12:57.056
founder and CEO of Meerkat.

00:12:59.026 --> 00:12:59.984
What's up my man?

00:12:59.984 --> 00:13:01.138
How many peeps in there?

00:13:01.138 --> 00:13:02.578
- [Voiceover] 339.

00:13:02.578 --> 00:13:04.121
- Three, three, nine.

00:13:04.121 --> 00:13:05.600
Awesome, cool!

